"""FROZEN mid-length prompts (~1k tokens each), hand-authored, diverse registers."""

PROMPTS_MID = [
("mid_fluoride", """Our regional water board is reopening the question of fluoride, and I have been asked to present the considerations at the next public meeting without advocating either side. Twenty years ago the utility adjusted naturally occurring fluoride in our wells up to roughly one milligram per liter, matching what most public health agencies recommended at the time. Since then, several countries have ended their programs, toothpaste formulations changed, a national survey reported slightly lower cavity rates than expected in unfluoridated neighboring districts, and a study associated very high natural fluoride in other regions with lower childhood test scores. Meanwhile our own dental charity reports that children in the poorest villages here, where families cannot afford checkups, still show high cavity rates, and older residents who grew up before adjustment remember losing teeth early.

Some background you should work with rather than around. Our wells carry about four-tenths of a milligram per liter naturally, the adjustment has been continuous since it began, and the utility's own records, which I have been shown in confidence, contain two gaps of several months during a staffing crisis eleven years ago where the dosing log is reconstructed from purchase records rather than measured readings. The neighboring unfluoridated district is not a clean comparison: it is wealthier on average, has twice our dentists per capita, fluoridates nothing but sells bottled water with unknown fluoride content to a quarter of households, and began a school varnish program five years before we did. Our dental charity's cavity figures come from clinics, which means the poorest villagers who never reach a clinic are invisible in them, and the charity's director, when I pressed her, agreed the denominator problem is real but said the direction of the effect has been consistent across six years. The angry parents, for their part, have compiled a dossier that mixes genuine studies with fabricated ones and at least two papers retracted for reasons unrelated to fluoride, and the proud dentists have circulated a pamphlet from the nineteen-seventies whose dosage recommendations have been superseded twice. Both sides have booked the same guest speaker for different meetings in the same week.

I also want help with the ethics of my own position, which the organizers have not thought through. I sit on the school's governing body with two of the angriest parents, I buy my vegetables from the shop run by one of the proud dentists, and the utility has offered me, unofficially, access to its engineers 'so the presentation is technically accurate', an offer whose helpfulness and whose leverage I can both feel. Whatever structure you propose should survive being delivered by a man whom half the room will suspect of being bought and the other half of being a coward, and should leave the audience, whatever they decide, with the sense that the decision was theirs and was made on the merits they care about. If the honest answer is that the evidence does not justify changing what we do, say how to present a non-change so that it does not read as an establishment shrug; if the honest answer is that the evidence justifies ending the adjustment, say how to present that without handing the meeting to the dossier's fabricators; and if the honest answer is that we genuinely do not know, give me the language for carrying uncertainty in front of people who have been told for twenty years that certainty was available. I would rather stand up with a defensible humility than a borrowed confidence, but I need to know what that costs, because I suspect the room will not forgive it evenly from both directions.

Walk me through how to structure this presentation honestly. Cover what the evidence fairly supports and where it is genuinely contested, the difference between dose and exposure in this debate, what questions I should ask our own utility about measurement and control, what alternatives exist such as varnish programs or milk schemes and what their track records look like, and how to present uncertainty to a room that will contain both angry parents and proud local dentists. I need the reasoning laid out, not a verdict."""),

("mid_bakernight", """I manage a small neighborhood bakery with four bakers and we are redesigning the night schedule. Production starts when the dough from the evening prep is ready, ovens run in two batches, and the shop opens at six in the morning. Our current rotation has each baker working five nights from nine at night until six or seven in the morning, with two days off that rotate. Complaints include falling asleep on the commute home, constant colds in winter, arguments about weekend nights, and one baker who has quietly asked to never work Sundays for family reasons. We also have a part-timer who can cover two nights a week but not consecutive ones.

Operational details that constrain any solution. The dough tolerates a two-hour window on either side of its planned bake, so one night's start time can move within limits but not float freely. The oven's first batch holds one hundred loaves and the second two hundred, and the second batch only pays if the shop's weekend trade justifies it, which the previous manager decided by rule of thumb and nobody has examined since. Deliveries to two cafés happen at six forty-five, before the shop opens, and whoever handles them has done the longest and least popular duty in the rota for three years because the rota was built around whoever volunteered first. One baker is fifty-eight and has had a shoulder operation that makes the two-hundred-loave batch shaping painful for two days afterward; another has small children and a partner who works days, so her sleep window is already squeezed at both ends; the youngest sleeps anywhere and claims the shifts do not affect him, but he has been late twice this quarter and the other bakers have noticed before I have. The shop closes at four in the afternoon, so a baker can theoretically get an evening nap before a nine o'clock start, though the two who have families say the theoretical nap does not survive contact with a household. Nobody has ever formally complained; the complaints reach me as remarks at the flour delivery, which is how bakeries talk, and I have to translate them into policy without betraying who said what.

What the team has already tried, so we do not re-tread it. For one season we ran fixed teams on fixed nights, which everyone liked for a month and then hated, because the fixed early-week team quietly became the junior team and the fixed weekend team burned out in a way that took the rest of us by surprise. We tried self-selecting shifts on a whiteboard, which worked for the confident and starved the quiet. The part-timer was brought in to relieve Sundays specifically, but her availability keeps drifting with her other job and the rota assumes a reliability she cannot always give, which produces a monthly scramble I end up solving by working the shift myself, and my own sleep, which was already the worst in the kitchen, has become a standing joke with a bitter center. We have an air mattress in the office that nobody uses because sleeping at work feels like admitting the schedule has beaten you, and there is a stack of unclaimed holiday forms by the time clock that tells me the team is tired in ways they will not put in writing. Whatever you design should be explainable on one side of paper, survive one member calling in sick, and make the load of the unpopular duties visible to everyone instead of absorbed invisibly by whoever is least able to refuse.

Design a better rotation and explain the reasoning. Address how to place the longest sleep block relative to the shift, how to rotate forward rather than backward across a month, how to handle the Sunday exemption without unfairness, how to use the part-timer to break up bad stretches, what the handover between the evening prep person and the night crew should contain, and what simple rules the team can self-enforce when someone is clearly exhausted. Where the science is thin or contested, say so plainly rather than inventing certainty."""),

("mid_organ", """Our town hall pipe organ is ninety years old and the borough has budgeted a fraction of what the two visiting builders quoted. The instrument has three manuals and pedalboard, electro-pneumatic action, and a history I will summarize. In the fifties the original tracker action was converted, in the eighties a solid-state relay replaced the pneumatic ones, and last winter a leak above the chamber soaked the north wall. Currently the great principal chorus sounds unstable in pitch when the hall is cold, about a fifth of the swell division notes speak late or not at all, the combination action has lost its memory, and the blower motor screams during the first twenty minutes of running. The congregation will not fundraise again for years after the roof campaign.

Quotation and history detail you should use. The two builders' figures differ by forty percent and so does their scope: the smaller quote covers the swell restoration and blower overhaul with the unstable chorus 'monitored for a season', while the larger covers the chorus re-regulation now, a new solid-state combination action, and a chamber dehumidifier, and its covering letter describes our north wall damp as 'the primary threat to instrument survival' without costing the wall work, which is the parish's responsibility and not theirs. The instrument was built by a firm that folded thirty years ago, so parts come from stock and skill rather than a manufacturer, though one of the quoting firms trained under that firm's last foreman. The hall is used for the town's concert series six times a year, two choral societies rehearse weekly, the school holds its carol service there, and the organ's unstable chorus has begun, according to the concert series' treasurer, to appear in program notes as a footnote about repertoire selection. The parish's reserves would be exhausted by the larger quote; the smaller quote plus the wall work can be afforded this year with about ten percent left over, and the roof campaign's final debt payment falls due in two years, after which the parish's fundraising capacity roughly doubles on the treasurer's projection, which depends on a housing development the planning committee has approved and a developer has not yet started.

Two further complications belong in the reasoning. The first is that the swell division's failing notes are not random: they cluster in the pipes that speak into the north wall's damp corner, and the builder who trained under the original firm told me privately that if the wall is not dealt with, any restoration of those pipes is a restoration of pipes sitting in a wet towel, which reframes the wall work from parish maintenance to instrument project and moves its cost, politically if not legally, onto the organ fund. The second is the question of what the congregation is actually asked to sit through during the work: the smaller quote's phased schedule keeps the organ playable throughout with the swell closed for a season, the larger quote's schedule takes the instrument down for a summer and part of autumn, and the choral societies have told me, through their directors with the delicacy of people negotiating a hostage release, that a silent autumn would move their rehearsals to the Methodist hall across the road, from which, historically, they have not returned. If the committee is to choose honestly between a cheap repair now that may be redone in ten years and an expensive repair now that buys thirty, it needs to understand not just the engineering of the two quotes but the institutional fragility around them, because the organ's enemies are not damp and dust but a concert series treasurer with a spreadsheet and two choral directors with car keys.

Help me rank the repairs and justify the order. Explain the likely cause and consequence of each fault, which ones are actively destroying value if left alone versus merely annoying, which can be safely deferred with monitoring and what the monitoring should be, how to judge whether a partial rebuild now versus a complete one later saves or wastes money, and what questions I should put to the two builders to compare their quotes fairly. If some advice conflicts between conservation practice and practical parish use, lay out the tension instead of hiding it."""),

("mid_joboffers", """I must choose between two jobs and keep going back and forth, so I want a clear-eyed comparison rather than reassurance. Offer one is a conservation science lab in a mid-sized inland city: analyzing pigments and adhesives for regional museums, a small permanent team, a respected but financially fragile institution, salary just above my current one, a forty-minute bus commute, and a director who has promised me the lead role on a major fresco project beginning next year if funding holds. Offer two is a field survey position with an environmental consultancy: mostly outdoor sampling campaigns across coastal sites, two weeks away per month, notably higher pay with a car allowance, a large company where promotions depend on utilization targets, and a manager I met once who seemed sharp but overstretched.

Personal context that should inform the weighing. My current position is in a university heritage science unit that has just been reorganized under a department head who does not believe in long-horizon research, and my own project portfolio has been shrinking accordingly; the move is happening to me whether I like it or not, so both offers arrived at a moment when staying is the option that actually requires justification. My partner runs a studio in the city where we live now and cannot relocate for two years without abandoning clients, so either offer means commuting in some form: the inland lab city is ninety minutes each way by train, the consultancy's field weeks would make the question moot for half of each month, which my partner has described, carefully, as 'perhaps easier than you think and harder than you hope'. Financially we are comfortable but carry a mortgage sized for two incomes that will become one for the two years of the consultancy's travel, unless we take it. I am thirty-four, my publication record is respectable but thin for a group leader position, my referees are strong, and the thing I actually want — running my own group — exists at both destinations in theory: the lab would grow me into it from inside a fragile institution, the consultancy has just created an internal research arm whose head is fourteen months from retirement, and its job posting, which I have read more times than I will admit, describes exactly the group I want to run, inside a company whose incentives I do not yet understand.

I should also confess the shape of my own temptation, because a fair analysis should know what it is arguing against. The consultancy's salary number arrived on a day the university unit announced its third restructuring in two years, and there is a version of me that wants to spend the next decade doing fieldwork in coastal wind with my hands dirty and my inbox empty on Fridays, and I do not know how much of that wanting is wisdom and how much is the particular exhaustion of having spent a decade in institutions that mistake reports for work. Against that, the lab's director, whom I have known since my doctorate, said something at the interview dinner that I have been turning over since: that a fragile institution is the only place where a person in their thirties gets handed a fresco project, a lab and a budget line all at once, because safe institutions distribute those things among people who already have them. She may of course be describing her own argument for staying, which is that she has said it to every candidate for a decade, and the fresco's funding, which she calls 'pending', is pending in the way that institutional funding is always pending. What I want from you is not reassurance in either direction but the actual mechanics of the choice: which option's risk I am qualified to carry, which one's regret is recoverable, and what I should be asking myself that I have not thought to ask.

Weigh them for me across the dimensions I should actually care about at my stage, which is ten years in and wanting eventually to lead my own group. Cover skill compounding and which environment builds rarer expertise, the real cost of travel-heavy work on relationships and side projects, what funding fragility does to a lab career versus what utilization targets do to a consultant's, questions I should ask each employer before signing, and the decision traps people in my position typically fall into. I will make the choice; I want the analysis to be unflinching."""),

("mid_driving", """My daughter is seventeen and learning to drive our old estate car, which has a manual gearbox. She has had six hours with an instructor on quiet roads and can start on a flat surface without stalling most of the time. This weekend we begin practicing on real routes, and I want a proper lesson plan I can follow over the next two months, because our relationship survives better with structure than with improvised commentary.

Context about the car and our situation, because the plan should fit it. The car is a twenty-year-old estate with a clutch that bites high, brakes that pull slightly left when cold, and no driving aids beyond mirrors and my right hand; the handbrake works on the rear drums and holds the church hill if applied properly, which is itself a lesson. She passed her theory test first attempt and has the hazard-perception instincts of somebody who has watched a lot of dashcam videos, which helps with some hazards and builds false confidence with others. Our sessions can run an hour on weekday evenings and two hours on weekend mornings; her instructor, whom she sees weekly, has told me the things he wants covered outside his lessons and the things he wants left to him, and the two lists have one overlap, the hill starts, which he wants taught by me and assessed by him, for reasons he explained and I agreed with. Her test is booked for the second week of April at the test center across town, which she has never driven to, and the routes around it include the ring-road junction she will find hardest, which I can show her but not, obviously, rehearse with her. My own instruction history is a handful of professional lessons eleven years ago and a lifetime of habits, two of which — coasting into junctions, and checking mirrors by feel rather than fact — I have been asked by my own conscience to fix before teaching anyone anything.

One more thing you should know, because it changes the tone the plan needs. Her mother taught her to ride a bike on this same estate and did it with a patience I have never once managed in a car, and the two of them have a running joke that the family's driving gene went down the maternal line and stopped at the clutch. My own father taught me in a formation of silence, white knuckles and one catastrophic argument on a dual carriageway that we did not properly recover from for years, and I have promised myself, out loud, in front of witnesses, that I will not reproduce it. So the plan's most important output is not the session sequence, which any syllabus could give me, but the style of the instruction: what a father says instead of what a father feels, how to praise progress that is real without inflating confidence that is fragile, how to absorb a mistake that is genuinely dangerous without transferring the fear, and how to end a session at the moment it stops working rather than the moment the schedule says. If the plan you write does that part well, the gearbox part will take care of itself; I can teach stalling. What I need taught is how to be the calm one in a moving box with my child at the wheel and my own history in the passenger seat.

Write the plan as a sequence of sessions with clear goals and honest exit criteria for moving on. Start from the first proper hill starts in our neighborhood with its steep church hill, then junctions of increasing difficulty, then the ring road at busier times, then rural roads with their blind crests and tractors, and finally night driving and rain before her test in early spring. For each stage describe what to demonstrate before she tries it, the exact words I should use instead of shouting, common failure modes and how to recover calmly from a stall in traffic or a wrong-lane mistake, and when to end a session early. Also cover how to handle my own habits, such as reaching for the imaginary brake, and how to make space for the fact that she will one day drive alone."""),

("mid_molasses", """Set the scene with what the city was, because the disaster's meaning depends on it: a port city in wartime and just after, with the armistice months old, the influenza's second wave receding, an anarchist bombing campaign that had put the whole country's police on edge months before, and a North End neighborhood crowded with immigrant families a few blocks from an industrial waterfront that the city's own inspectors had not visited in living memory. The company that owned the tank was one of the largest sugar refiners in the country, its local operations ran through a subsidiary whose managers were few and far between, and the tank had been built hurriedly, during a wartime sugar shortage, by an engineer whose qualifications the later trial would examine in detail and whose calculations, when they finally surfaced, told a story of their own. I have read the popular retellings, which disagree with each other on everything from the wave's height to the number of dead, and a couple of modern engineering papers that analyze the tank's failure, which agree with each other more than with the popular accounts; where the popular story and the engineering record conflict, I want the conflict named rather than smoothed over.

For the engineering section, go beyond the tank itself to the system that let it stand. The city's inspection regime at the time, such as it was; the wartime pressures that accelerated construction and deferred scrutiny; the industry practice of testing tanks with water, which this tank's critics said was skipped or shortened, and what a water test would and would not have revealed about a tank meant to hold a liquid a third denser; the engineering profession's own reckoning afterward, including the lawsuit's expert witnesses and what their fight did to the then-young practice of consulting engineering; and how modern failure analysis reconstructs the event from the physical evidence — the bent elevated track structure, the shards' thickness measurements, the rivet patterns recovered from the site — since the tank itself was dismantled and sold for scrap within months. I am told the steel's thickness ran roughly half of what the era's own standards would have wanted for that height and load, and I want you to explain, carefully, whether that claim survives modern scrutiny, because it is the single fact most quoted about the case and the one I most want to get right.

Tell me the full story of the Great Molasses Flood in Boston in nineteen nineteen as a piece of careful narrative history, then switch into an engineer's voice and draw out the lessons. For the narrative I want the sequence of the day itself, the tank and the company that owned it, the neighborhood it stood in, the warning signs beforehand including what residents noticed and what the company ignored, the wave's behavior through the streets, the rescue effort, and the litigation that followed with what it changed about how cities held companies accountable. For the engineering part, explain in accessible terms why the tank failed the way the evidence suggests: the thin steel, the untested riveted seams, the temperature of the fresh molasses versus the cold afternoon, and why the failure mode produced a wave rather than a simple spill. Then connect both halves to what a modern tank inspector or plant safety engineer should take from the case, and note where popular retellings exaggerate or get the physics wrong."""),

("mid_pond", """Last spring I dug a backyard pond roughly four meters across and a meter deep at the deepest point, lined it, and stocked it with a dozen goldfish and a handful of native minnows. Through summer it turned green, by early autumn the green cleared and string algae took over the waterfall, in winter it partly froze over with aeration running, and now in spring the fish seem healthy but there is a smell of rot near the shallow shelf where leaves collected. A heron visits most mornings. I have no filter, just the waterfall pump, plants on the shelf, and whatever the ecosystem does.

Facts I have measured or observed that may matter. The pond holds about thirty-two thousand liters by my arithmetic from the dig, the waterfall pump moves two thousand liters an hour up a meter of head, and the shallow shelf averages fifteen centimeters deep over roughly a third of the pond's area, which is where the water warms first and where the leaves collect. The liner is rubber over sand over clay. There is no bottom drain; the pump intake sits in a skimmer box at the surface on the windward side, which catches floating debris well in easterly winds and not at all in westerlies, and the pond sits in a garden open to the west with two birches at the west fence and a leylandii hedge to the north. I fed the fish through the frost-free months until February, then stopped; the heron takes perhaps one fish a week by my count of the disturbance marks; the goldfish bred twice last summer, so the population question is not academic; and the pH, which I started measuring in March with strips from the pond shop, reads between eight and eight and a half in the morning, higher after noon, which the shop assistant called normal for our water but which I would like explained rather than called normal.

My own theories, which you should test rather than indulge, are three. First, that the green phase was a nutrient problem whose sources were the fish food, the leaf fall I did not net out in autumn, and whatever washes off the lawn when it rains, and that the waterfall pump's turnover is too slow to matter either way. Second, that the string algae owns the waterfall because the waterfall is the only high-flow, high-light surface in the pond and everything else is too deep or too shaded for it, so the fight is not chemical but architectural: I am growing algae a perfect wall to live on and then resenting it. Third, that the shelf smell is the accumulated leaf mat composting anaerobically under the shallow warm water, and that the fix is mechanical removal and a deeper shelf edge, not another bottle from the pond shop, whose assistant has sold me four bottles whose combined effect I cannot distinguish from doing nothing. If any of these is wrong or half right, tell me where the reasoning breaks; if the truth is messier than three tidy theories, I would rather have the mess than the theory, because the pond has survived my theories for a year and I would like it to survive them for twenty.

Explain what happened through each season and why, in the order the causes actually chain together. Then design a low-intervention management plan for this year: what to do about the leaf shelf and the smell, how to deal with the string algae without nuking the pond with chemicals, which plants to add or thin and where, whether the fish population is sustainable at this size, realistic heron deterrence that does not ruin the view, and what warning signs next summer would tell me the system is tipping toward another green phase before it happens. Prefer moves that let the pond mostly manage itself."""),
]
